From 3f38c1c89da266868c92d334d9ecc2470fcb6558 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pasarguard <dev@pasarguard.local>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 05:17:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(clashapi): replace an inbound's users without restarting

A panel adds and removes users continuously. Restarting sing-box for each
change drops every live connection, so in practice user churn and sing-box
were incompatible.

The capability was already here: every protocol service supports having its
credential table replaced. Only the inbounds never exposed a way to ask for it
after startup, so it could happen once, inside NewInbound, and never again.

Three parts:

  hysteria2 gains an exported UpdateUsers. The service's credentials and
  userNameList are swapped under one write lock, because that list is indexed
  by the user id the service returns - updating them separately bills traffic
  arriving mid-swap to the wrong user. Name lookup is now bounds-checked: the
  id can come from a connection authenticated against a longer list, and
  indexing past the end panics the process.

  clash_api gains PUT /inbounds/{tag}/users, behind the existing secret. It is
  generic rather than per-protocol - the remaining six protocols are a case in
  one switch, not another route. The body matches the inbound's own users
  block, so whatever writes the config can drive this too.

  v2ray_api stats accept "*" in the users list, meaning count everyone. Naming
  users individually cannot work here: that list is read once at startup, so a
  user created later passes traffic attributed to nobody - which defeats the
  point of adding them without a restart. This matches how xray gates user
  stats, with a switch rather than an allowlist.

Verified against a live tunnel: a user created after startup passes traffic and
is counted under their own name, with no restart in the log.

Not covered: removing a user does not close their established sessions. New
connections are refused; existing ones run until they close.
